+# Full study: the power-product claim for 2-increasing triples
+
+## Frozen target
+
+At Formal Conjectures revision
+`2270d31e8dd611521f979de6d86da364930b7669`, the frozen declaration is
+
+```lean
+theorem maximalLength_pow {n : ℕ} {e : ℝ} (hn : 1 < n)
+    (h : F n = (n : ℝ) ^ e) :
+    ∀ᶠ m : ℕ in Filter.atTop, (m : ℝ) ^ e ≤ F m
+```
+
+Here `F` is the maximum length of a pairwise 2-increasing list of triples
+whose coordinates lie in `[1,n]`.  `Statement.lean` expands only this local
+notation and preserves the implicit binders, both hypotheses, real power,
+inequality, and `Filter.Eventually` conclusion.
+
+## Anchored reconstruction and cut analysis
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:BEGIN -->
+### PU-00 — identity and exact type
+
+The workset row, provider revision, source byte range, raw-block digest,
+declaration digest, formal-type digest, Stage5 identity, and Stage6 alias all
+agree.  The local definitions `lt₂`, `IsIncreasing₂`, and `maximalLength`
+replay the source definitions.  `Target` abbreviates `SourceStatement`, so
+`source_to_target` and `target_to_source` are identity transports without a
+change of binder or proposition.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-00:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:BEGIN -->
+### PU-01 — combinatorial object
+
+For triples `a,b : Fin 3 → ℕ`, `lt₂ a b` means that two distinct coordinates
+strictly increase.  A list is `IsIncreasing₂` when every earlier member is
+`lt₂` every later member.  `maximalLength n` is the natural supremum of the
+lengths of such lists whose coordinate ranges are contained in `Set.Icc 1 n`.
+This is the same extremal function used by the source paper.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-01:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:BEGIN -->
+### PU-02 — published product argument
+
+The paper forms lexicographically ordered products of admissible sequences.
+From an optimal sequence of length `F(n)=n^e`, its `k`-fold product is an
+admissible sequence on alphabet size `n^k` with length `n^(k e)`.  This gives
+witnesses at the unbounded subsequence `m=n^k`.  The paper states the result
+as “there are arbitrarily large `m`” and then separately derives the weaker
+all-`m` estimate `F(m) ≥ (m/n)^e`, and hence exponent `β<e` eventually.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-02:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:BEGIN -->
+### PU-03 — the frozen formal quantifier
+
+For naturals, `∀ᶠ m in Filter.atTop, P m` unfolds to the existence of a
+threshold `M` such that every `m≥M` satisfies `P m`.  This is stronger than
+the paper's unbounded-witness conclusion, which is
+`∀ N, ∃ m, N≤m ∧ P m`.  The distinction concerns all indices between product
+powers and is not a notation-only transport.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-03:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:BEGIN -->
+### PU-04 — valid one-way comparison
+
+`target_implies_arbitrarilyLarge` is a trust-zero Lean proof of the valid
+direction.  Given an eventual threshold `M` and a requested lower bound `N`,
+choose `max N M`; it is at least both thresholds and therefore supplies an
+arbitrarily large witness.  This checks that the frozen proposition would
+imply the prose claim, but it cannot be reversed generically.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-04:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:BEGIN -->
+### PU-05 — executable quantifier separation
+
+`EvenWitness m := ∃ k, m=2*k` holds at arbitrarily large natural indices:
+for any `N`, choose `2*N`.  It is not eventually true, since above any
+purported threshold the odd number `2*M+1` is a counterexample.  Lean checks
+both facts and packages them in `arbitrarilyLarge_does_not_imply_eventually`.
+Thus no logic-only step can turn PU-02 into the frozen conclusion.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-05:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:BEGIN -->
+### PU-06 — exact machine-root cut
+
+An exact root would have to prove the eventual lower bound for the particular
+extremal function, not merely the generic converse rejected by PU-05.  The
+pinned provider body is unavailable as evidence because its axiom census
+contains `sorryAx`.  The published product construction proves only the
+unbounded subsequence claim and the constant-loss/all-smaller-exponent
+corollaries.  No reviewed source anchor closes the stronger eventual-all cut.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-06:END -->
+
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:BEGIN -->
+### PU-07 — frontier handoff and release decision
+
+The exact statement and transports compile, and the quantifier audit is
+kernel-checked at trust level zero.  PU-06 remains an explicit machine cut:
+there is no declaration of the frozen target.  PU-07 provides the complete
+readable frontier and records a provisional handoff for Master review; it does
+not convert the source prose, a conditional implication, or a diagnostic
+dossier into mathematical closure.
+<!-- ANCHOR:PU-07:END -->
+
+## Trust boundary
+
+The three local Lean modules compile from a fresh output directory with
+`--trust=0`.  The audited declarations use only `propext`,
+`Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound` transitively.  The provider theorem body
+is not imported, and no claim-specific axiom, unsafe declaration, bodyless
+oracle, or placeholder occurs.  Because no exact target root exists, this
+clean audit is an M0-L candidate frontier rather than exact theorem closure.
